window.docContents[72].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--The-affine-Burgers-solution___-step-by-step":{"contents":"Burgers's equation with affine data  is solved by\n for as long as the denominator does not vanish. The\ndenominator is also the derivative of the characteristic position with respect\nto its initial footpoint. Its nonvanishing is therefore the geometric\ncondition that lets us recover a unique footpoint from the current position.\nThe division in the formula and the invertibility of characteristics carry\nthe same restriction.\n\nThe affine-solution theorem\nfollows by differentiating on  and checking that\n:\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (0 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x =\n      -a * burgersNumer a c x * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (1 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x =\n      a * (burgersDenom a x)⁻¹ := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\nopen BurgersEquation PDE.Common in\nopen PDE (SpaceTime partialDeriv) in\nexample (a c : ℝ) (x : SpaceTime 1) (hden : burgersDenom a x ≠ 0) :\n    partialDeriv (0 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x +\n        burgersAffineSolution a c x *\n          partialDeriv (1 : Fin 2) (burgersAffineSolution a c) x = 0 := by\n  unfold burgersAffineSolution burgersNumer burgersInvDenom burgersDenom\n  unfold burgersDenom at hden\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.4. The affine Burgers solution, step by step","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--The-affine-Burgers-solution___-step-by-step"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Convective-acceleration-and-Bernoulli___s-law":{"contents":"At a point where every velocity component is differentiable, the gradient\nand antisymmetric parts of the velocity derivative separate convective\nacceleration into kinetic-energy and vorticity terms. With\n and\n,\n\n\n\nSet . At a point where the velocity components and pressure are\ndifferentiable, the steady, unforced balance\n givesThus the Bernoulli function is constant along streamlines on which these\nhypotheses hold. If the flow is also irrotational at the point, thenThe Euler balance then gives the stronger vector identityThe theorem is pointwise; constancy of the Bernoulli function on a connected\nregion follows when these derivatives vanish throughout that region.\n\nPair the steady momentum balance\n with . The convective-power identity gives\n, hence\n.For the irrotational strengthening, expand the vorticity contraction:The derivative of  is , so those terms cancel\nand leave . If the vorticity components vanish,\nthe steady Euler component\n becomes\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.5. Convective acceleration and Bernoulli's law","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Convective-acceleration-and-Bernoulli___s-law"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction":{"contents":"Finite-dimensional trial spaces turn the weak PDE into a system of\nCarathéodory ODEs with a pivot mass operator. Stability of the projections in\nboth  and , together with density, lets their solutions approximate the\ninfinite-dimensional problem.\n\nThe construction has four distinct stages:\n\n1. restrict the form and load to each trial space and solve the finite system;2. derive energy bounds independent of the trial dimension;3. extract a weak state limit and pass to the integrated identity;4. use the energy argument to identify the unique limit.\n\nThe quantitative time-Sobolev estimate turns Lions–Magenes regularity into a\nbound on the continuous representative; the parabolic regularity theorems and\nthe weak wave theory use it to control time-dependent forcing and\ndifferentiated Galerkin families. When the energy-to-pivot inclusion is\ncompact, the Aubin–Lions theorem combines that time regularity with the\nspectral Galerkin tail estimate to produce strong compactness in\n.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.4. Galerkin Construction","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Other-PDE-libraries-and-geometric-flow":{"contents":"Soto Franco and Marshall-Stevens's\nEllipticPDE\nformalizes linear divergence-form Dirichlet theory\nwith a companion paper.\nIts named results cover weak solvability, Gårding's inequality, Fredholm theory,\nspectral compactness, and interior regularity. This overlaps directly with\nPDEAtlas's variational and regularity layers. Their work concentrates on linear\nelliptic solvability, while PDEAtlas follows a wider textbook progression and\nconnects that analysis to evolution equations and certified calculations.\nThe useful compatibility questions concern their Sobolev-space definitions,\ncoefficient assumptions, and the passage from a weak solution to a regular\nrepresentative. A Hölder conclusion obtained from smooth-coefficient Sobolev\nregularity has different hypotheses from De Giorgi–Nash–Moser theory.\n\nQin, Liao, Khaitan and Chow's\nPoincaré formalization\nfollows Hamilton–Perelman geometric analysis:\ntheir paper describes Ricci flow with surgery\nand finite-time extinction leading to the smooth and topological Poincaré\nconjectures. This is PDE formalization in a geometric setting, with evolving\nmetrics and curvature. PDEAtlas's present exposition concerns Euclidean-domain\nanalysis. Sharing estimates, compactness or evolution arguments would require\nconnecting our Euclidean objects to the geometric objects and hypotheses of\nthat project.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.2. Other PDE libraries and geometric flow","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Other-PDE-libraries-and-geometric-flow"}});